1
00:00:00,896 --> 00:00:08,264
This is Sprintboard, a multi-tenant project tracker in the style of Jira, built for the Nexvia full-stack internship.

2
00:00:08,648 --> 00:00:16,928
Every company gets its own private workspace, with projects, a Kanban board, sprints, custom workflows, and billing through Stripe.

3
00:00:17,324 --> 00:00:19,196
Let me show you around.

4
00:00:19,591 --> 00:00:23,263
I'll sign in as the owner of a demo company called Acme.

5
00:00:26,675 --> 00:00:33,875
Acme is one of three seeded companies. When I switch workspace, every project and issue changes with it.

6
00:00:34,262 --> 00:00:45,278
Behind the scenes, each request carries the workspace in a header, and the API returns a not-found for any workspace you don't belong to. So one company can never see another's data.

7
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:51,228
Here's the board for one of Acme's projects. The columns come from the project's own workflow.

8
00:00:51,620 --> 00:01:00,812
Dragging a card changes its status. The order inside a column is stored as a rank, so a move writes a single row, instead of renumbering the whole column.

9
00:01:01,206 --> 00:01:06,654
If the workflow doesn't allow a move, the drop is rejected and the card stays where it was.

10
00:01:07,044 --> 00:01:14,100
Opening a card shows every field, its comments and attachments, and a full history of who changed what, and when.

11
00:01:14,494 --> 00:01:19,198
You can filter the board as you type, and slash jumps straight to the filter box.

12
00:01:19,585 --> 00:01:22,993
Pressing C creates an issue from anywhere.

13
00:01:24,610 --> 00:01:32,794
The backlog groups issues by sprint. Search uses Postgres full text search, or you can type an issue key to jump straight to it.

14
00:01:33,175 --> 00:01:38,959
Sprints can be started and completed, and finishing one sends unfinished work back to the backlog.

15
00:01:39,342 --> 00:01:45,750
Admins design each project's workflow, with their own statuses and the transitions that are allowed between them.

16
00:01:46,130 --> 00:01:54,530
Completing a sprint queues a Celery job that builds a report with a CSV download. A weekly summary also goes out by email every Monday.

17
00:01:54,923 --> 00:02:00,539
Workspace settings hold the members, with owner, admin, member and viewer roles.

18
00:02:00,927 --> 00:02:07,839
Webhooks are signed with an HMAC, retried with backoff, and blocked from calling private network addresses.

19
00:02:08,230 --> 00:02:13,702
Owners can also export everything as JSON, or delete the workspace entirely.

20
00:02:14,090 --> 00:02:20,834
Security got a lot of attention. Passwords can be reset by email, and email addresses are verified.

21
00:02:21,221 --> 00:02:27,773
Two-factor authentication works with any authenticator app, and comes with eight single-use recovery codes.

22
00:02:28,156 --> 00:02:33,724
Refresh tokens rotate on every use, and signing out or changing your password revokes them.

23
00:02:34,108 --> 00:02:38,452
After five wrong passwords, sign-in locks for fifteen minutes.

24
00:02:54,653 --> 00:03:01,925
Uploads are filtered by file type and can be virus scanned, and everything sits behind rate limits and strict security headers.

25
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:07,710
Now billing. I'll sign in as the owner of Initech, which is on the free plan.

26
00:03:09,051 --> 00:03:20,859
The billing page shows the plan, the limits, and how much of each is used. The API enforces them, so going over a limit returns a payment-required response, which the app turns into an upgrade prompt.

27
00:03:21,239 --> 00:03:28,007
With a Stripe key set, the upgrade buttons are live. Choosing Pro opens Stripe's hosted checkout page.

28
00:03:29,052 --> 00:03:38,556
This is where you would enter the test card. To keep the demo hands-free, I'll complete the subscription with Stripe's test payment method instead, and let the webhook do the rest.

29
00:03:45,161 --> 00:03:52,625
The workspace is now on Pro, and the limits have lifted. Cancelling the subscription sends it back to the free plan.

30
00:03:57,871 --> 00:04:03,439
Payments run through Stripe in test mode. Here's how to connect your own account in a few minutes.

31
00:04:04,108 --> 00:04:15,172
Step one. Create a free Stripe account, switch on test mode, and copy the secret key that starts with s k underscore test. Put it in the backend env file.

32
00:04:15,825 --> 00:04:24,057
Step two. Run the stripe setup command. It creates the Pro and Business prices, and it refuses to run against a live key.

33
00:04:24,703 --> 00:04:36,991
Step three. Install the Stripe command line tool, log in, and forward webhooks to the API. It prints a signing secret that starts with w h sec. Add that to the env file too.

34
00:04:37,639 --> 00:04:43,687
Step four. In the Stripe dashboard, open the customer portal settings and save them once.

35
00:04:44,334 --> 00:04:50,934
Step five. Restart the API and the worker, open the billing page as an owner, and choose Pro.

36
00:04:51,588 --> 00:05:01,548
Step six. On Stripe's checkout page, pay with the test card four two four two, repeated four times, with any future date and any security code.

37
00:05:01,940 --> 00:05:07,004
Another card is always declined, and a third one triggers three D secure.

38
00:05:07,653 --> 00:05:16,773
Step seven. The webhook arrives, the workspace flips to Pro, and the limits lift. Cancel from the portal, and it drops back to free.

39
00:05:18,582 --> 00:05:24,678
The layout works on a phone too. The sidebar becomes a menu, and the board scrolls sideways.

40
00:05:28,684 --> 00:05:39,772
Under the hood, every change is written to an audit log. After the transaction commits, one Celery task turns each entry into notifications and webhook deliveries.

41
00:05:40,422 --> 00:05:49,326
Tenancy uses a shared schema with a workspace id on every table, enforced by a base viewset, and covered by cross-tenant tests.

42
00:05:49,975 --> 00:05:58,879
GitHub Actions runs linting, the backend tests, the browser tests, dependency audits, and builds the Docker images, then deploys to staging or production.

43
00:05:59,723 --> 00:06:05,747
In total there are one hundred eighteen backend tests, six unit tests, and thirty nine browser tests.

44
00:06:06,661 --> 00:06:14,989
That's Sprintboard. The code, the architecture notes, the API docs and the test results are all in the repository. Thanks for watching.
